WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.690
Off Croatia, some 1,200 islands
and islets line the coast:

2
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.830
long, and side by side.

3
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:12.190
On the Baltic, the Curonian
Spit runs for 98 kilometres,

4
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.770
a thin strip of sand
between sea and lagoon.

5
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:20.390
In Dorset, the coast swings
in and out: Durlston Head,

6
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.430
Swanage Bay, Ballard Point, Studland Bay.

7
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.590
Islands, spits, headlands and bays.

8
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.670
So why do coasts take
such different shapes?

9
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.450
In this mission, you'll
map the littoral zone,

10
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.090
from the storm-only backshore
out to the offshore,

11
00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.110
and see how fast it can change.

12
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:44.110
Then you'll classify coasts, using
long-term and short-term criteria.

13
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.430
Your mission: find out why coasts
take such different shapes.
